A||||7||varnster||to many amps, bigger alternator or a cap?||||1||
Z||000000||varnster||05-05-2001||09:59 PM||||he we go! im pushing a 100x4 and a 125x1, <BR>pioneer din and a half, 6 disc changer,and a<BR>8" jl audio sub, remote start, power locks and trunk, running about a mile of 8 guage cable from trunk to the amps located in the spare tire hole.When I crank the stereo(sounds great) with car running and activate<BR>a power source(locks, trunk etc.) the car shuts down, but if the stero is at normal level and the batt is charged all works fine, which rules out a short or ground. so maybe adding a capacitor will do the trick or upgrading the altenator or do both? what is your opinion?<BR>I dont want to waste money. by the way the car has a stock 2.8 alt on it.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||05-05-2001||10:17 PM||||Stock.... doesn't mean much. could be any one of 3-4 diferant units. You need to find out which one.<P>You'll need to check over the entire system. not just the stereo. Everything needs to be perfect.<P>When you hit the locks you create a current spike that is probably crashing the computer and killing the engine.<P>You may need an alternator, and a big cap, but first you need to check all the power and ground leads. You are probably operating them near capacity and one of them may be loose or weak, which could easily cause your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||05-05-2001||10:20 PM||||Varnster, have you thought of putting the battery up front?  Then you would only have to run some 1/0 cable from the engine to the battery, and your amps would be right next to the battery to hook up to.  Also, what kind of battery are you using?  A high c.c.a. battery will help a lot, such as an Optima gel cell (I'm a huge supporter of this battery).  A capacitor will help, but the earlier suggestions should be given some thought.  Also, make sure if you buy a higher capacity alternator it has increased cooling capacity as well.  Otherwise, you'll be replacing/rebuilding it a lot.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||varnster||05-05-2001||11:19 PM||||I thought all stock 2.8 alts were 96amp(on<BR>the fiero) I have heard alot of good things <BR>about thase batts you spoke of, now I have<BR>a WALLYMART everstart. not to great I know but it came with the car, so you think just replacing the battery will do it while leaving the stock alt. allso how can you tell<BR>the amperage rating on the alternator without pulling it.thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||05-06-2001||12:24 AM||||I'm told Wallmart bateries are made by Johnson Controls. Same people make Sears security battery and allot of others. They are good product. I use them myself.<P>In your case you want the highest capacity battery you can fit int he hole.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||05-06-2001||01:31 AM||||My thing is, though, that you aren't pulling all that much juice. You should be able to run all that on a stock 97Amp alternator and a half-decent battery. <BR>Can you get the car to stall consistently? If so, try keeping the revs up at about 2500rpm, and try to get it to stall. It probably won't. In that case, your alternator is probably shot. <BR>I had the same problem with my mazda. As it turned out, the alternator wouldn't even push enough juice to charge the system at idle. Past 1100Rpm, though, it was fine. <BR>Get the alternator checked out.||||reg||6
Z||000006||Nashco||05-06-2001||02:03 AM||||The alternator and battery are funny, in that if either one is run for a long time without working properly, the other will usually go bad too.  Perhaps your crappy battery has been drained too much by all the accessories, and as a result has overworked the alternator and caused it to finally start crapping out.  This is just an idea, but something to think about.  I highly highly recommend the Optima in any case...it doesn't do any harm, that's for damn sure.  It's got a two year full warranty, but mine's lasted almost three years without a sign of slowing down.  You can discharge the hell out of them and they still go.  Just make sure to test your entire system if you replace something.  If you get a battery, check you charging system afterwards.  If you get an alternator, check the battery while you're at it.  With what you're running, a properly operating system should work fine.  Good luck.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||mckay_lee||05-06-2001||11:14 PM||||I had the same problem in my car. It turned out to be wore out brushes on the alternator, so it would barly charge the battery. I also highly reccomend the optima battery, because you just cant kill one. I have 2 of them in my fiero one for the car and one for the sound system. ||||reg||1
